findings.body:
`write_and_make_executable` now calls `fs::set_permissions` before `fs::write`. On a fresh tools directory the target file does not exist yet, so `set_permissions` returns ENOENT and the function returns `FileWriteError::Permissions`. That error propagates through `?` for AppRun, the linuxdeploy AppImage, and the GStreamer script, so the whole bundle step fails on any machine where the cache is empty. The old order (write, then chmod) worked, and nothing in the diff explains the swap.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-315"}

Swap the two calls back so the file is written first and chmod'ed second. If the goal was to avoid a window where the file is readable before it is executable, create it with `OpenOptions::new().mode(0o770)` and then write, which keeps the security property without the ordering bug.

`prepare_tools` returns `crate::Result<PathBuf>`, and each call site uses `write_and_make_executable(...)?`. Before the PR the helper returned `io::Error`, which converts through `Error::IoError(#[from] io::Error)` in `crates/tauri-bundler/src/error.rs`. `FileWriteError` has no `From` impl and no `#[from]` variant covers it, so each `?` is error E0277 (`From<FileWriteError>` is not implemented for `Error`). I could not run `cargo check` in this environment, so this is from reading the code. The compile error should be easy to confirm with `cargo check -p tauri-bundler`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="232-232"}

Add either a `From<FileWriteError> for crate::Error` impl or a variant on `crate::Error` that wraps it, or have `write_and_make_executable` return `crate::Result<()>` and map each error into the existing `Error::Fs { context, path, error }` variant, which already carries the path. The last option is also the most useful for users, since the error message will name the file.

The GStreamer and other guards use `if !x.exists()`, but the GTK guard reads `if gtk.exists()`. On a fresh cache, `gtk` is absent, the block is skipped, and `linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh` is never written. Linuxdeploy then cannot run the GTK plugin, so GTK resources are not bundled for Tauri apps. On a machine that already has the file, the script gets overwritten on every run, which hides the bug from anyone testing on a warm cache.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="242-246"}

Change the guard back to `if !gtk.exists()`.

The base code mapped i686 to `i383` for the linuxdeploy artifact name and used that same `linuxdeploy_arch` for both the local file name and the download URL. The PR keeps `linuxdeploy_arch` for the local file name but changes the URL to `linuxdeploy-{arch}.AppImage`. For i686, the download now requests `linuxdeploy-i686.AppImage`, while the cache file is still `linuxdeploy-i383.AppImage`. This is not in the PR description, the changelog, or the commit message. It may fix a real naming mismatch in the release bucket, but if the release only has `i383` assets, i686 users will get a 404 on the linuxdeploy download.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="238-238"}

Either revert the URL to `{linuxdeploy_arch}` or split it into its own change with a note. If the `i686` asset actually exists, add a comment and a changelog line.
